{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-320686" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the points where the curve $x=4t,\\,y=\\frac{32}{t}$ intersects the line $\\frac{1}{2}x-y=0.$\n\nThe intersection points are $(-16,$\n{{field-1}}$)$ and $(${{field-2}}$,$\n{{field-3}}$).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061747/state.json" "file_sha256" "d281b7ac55f639ff79c2cc4ab3e0afac3e4094c82cd4d675fb90db09617d406f"} {"question" "q-320686" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the coordinates of intersection points, we take the equation of the line, replace $x$ and $y$ with the parametric formulas, and then solve for $t.$ Upon finding $t,$ we then find the coordinates of the intersection points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}x-y & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(4t)-\\frac{32}{t} & =0 \\\\ 2t-\\frac{32}{t} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{2{t}^{2}-32}{t} & =0 \\\\ 2{t}^{2}-32 & =0 \\\\ 2({t}^{2}-16) & =0 \\\\ 2(t+4)(t-4) & =0 \\\\ t & =\\pm 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we have the values of $t,$ we proceed to find $x$ and $y.$ \n\n- We start with $t=-4.$ For $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =4t \\\\  & =4(-4) \\\\  & =-16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{32}{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{(-4)} \\\\  & =-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the coordinates of the first intersection point are $(-16,-8).$\n\n- We now move over to $t=4.$ For $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =4t \\\\  & =4(4) \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{32}{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{4} \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the coordinates of the second intersection point are $(16,8).$\n\nTherefore, the curve $x=4t,\\,y=\\frac{32}{t}$ intersects the line $\\frac{1}{2}x-y=0$ at the points $(-16,-8)$ and $(16,8).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061747/state.json" "file_sha256" "d281b7ac55f639ff79c2cc4ab3e0afac3e4094c82cd4d675fb90db09617d406f"} {"question" "q-320686" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061747/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d67c2f25f9576853d5e88974fffe7258946afecb8c5dacf44c2234f506d47f32"} {"question" "q-320686" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061747/state.json" "file_sha256" "d281b7ac55f639ff79c2cc4ab3e0afac3e4094c82cd4d675fb90db09617d406f"} {"question" "q-320686" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-8"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061747/state.json" "file_sha256" "d281b7ac55f639ff79c2cc4ab3e0afac3e4094c82cd4d675fb90db09617d406f"} {"question" "q-320686" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061747/state.json" "file_sha256" "d281b7ac55f639ff79c2cc4ab3e0afac3e4094c82cd4d675fb90db09617d406f"} {"question" "q-320686" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "16"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061747/state.json" "file_sha256" "d281b7ac55f639ff79c2cc4ab3e0afac3e4094c82cd4d675fb90db09617d406f"} {"question" "q-320686" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061747/state.json" "file_sha256" "d281b7ac55f639ff79c2cc4ab3e0afac3e4094c82cd4d675fb90db09617d406f"} {"question" "q-320686" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "8"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061747/state.json" "file_sha256" "d281b7ac55f639ff79c2cc4ab3e0afac3e4094c82cd4d675fb90db09617d406f"} {"question" "q-274671" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061747/assets/3ec88af84d96caed2c27b9b1596b5320ff7ceaaac71ce12e48623c2be52f18ba.png)\n\nIn **ascending order**, the values of $t$ at which the curve $x=t+6,\\,y=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}-4$ intersects the line $y=\\frac{1}{2}x-1$ (shown above) are\n\n$t=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,t=$\n{{field-2}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061747/state.json" "file_sha256" "d281b7ac55f639ff79c2cc4ab3e0afac3e4094c82cd4d675fb90db09617d406f"} {"question" "q-274671" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that the line and curve intersect twice, so we expect two solutions.\n\nTo find the coordinates of the intersection points, we take the equation of the line, replace $x$ and $y$ with the parametric formulas, and then solve for $t.$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{2}x-1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}-4 & =\\frac{1}{2}(t+6)-1 \\\\ {t}^{2}-8 & =(t+6)-2 \\\\ {t}^{2}-8 & =t+4 \\\\ {t}^{2}-t-12 & =0 \\\\ (t+3)(t-4) & =0 \\\\ t & =-3,\\,4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the values of $t$ where the curve $x=t+6,\\,y=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}-4$ intersects the line $y=\\frac{1}{2}x-1$ are $t=-3$ and $t=4.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061747/state.json" "file_sha256" "d281b7ac55f639ff79c2cc4ab3e0afac3e4094c82cd4d675fb90db09617d406f"} {"question" "q-274671" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061747/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d67c2f25f9576853d5e88974fffe7258946afecb8c5dacf44c2234f506d47f32"} {"question" "q-274671" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061747/state.json" "file_sha256" "d281b7ac55f639ff79c2cc4ab3e0afac3e4094c82cd4d675fb90db09617d406f"} {"question" "q-274671" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-3"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061747/state.json" "file_sha256" "d281b7ac55f639ff79c2cc4ab3e0afac3e4094c82cd4d675fb90db09617d406f"} {"question" "q-274671" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061747/state.json" "file_sha256" "d281b7ac55f639ff79c2cc4ab3e0afac3e4094c82cd4d675fb90db09617d406f"} {"question" "q-274671" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061747/state.json" "file_sha256" "d281b7ac55f639ff79c2cc4ab3e0afac3e4094c82cd4d675fb90db09617d406f"} {"question" "q-274673" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the points where the curve $x={t}^{2},\\,y=4t$ intersects the line $x+y+3=0.$\n\nThe intersection points are $(1,$\n{{field-1}}$)$ and $(${{field-2}}$,$\n{{field-3}}$).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061747/state.json" "file_sha256" "d281b7ac55f639ff79c2cc4ab3e0afac3e4094c82cd4d675fb90db09617d406f"} {"question" "q-274673" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the coordinates of intersection points, we take the equation of the line, replace $x$ and $y$ with the parametric formulas, and then solve for $t.$ Upon finding $t,$ we then find the coordinates of the intersection points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y+3 & =0 \\\\ {t}^{2}+4t+3 & =0 \\\\ (t+3)(t+1) & =0 \\\\ t & =-3,-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we have the values of $t,$ we proceed to find $x$ and $y:$ \n\n- We start with $t=-3.$ For $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={t}^{2} \\\\  & =(-3)^{2} \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =4t \\\\  & =4(-3) \\\\  & =-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the coordinates of the first intersection point are $(9,-12).$\n\n- We now move over to $t=-1.$ For $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={t}^{2} \\\\  & =(-1)^{2} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =4t \\\\  & =4(-1) \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the coordinates of the second intersection point are $(1,-4).$\n\nTherefore, the curve $x={t}^{2},y=4t$ intersects the line $x+y+3=0$ at the points $(1,-4)$ and $(9,-12).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061747/state.json" "file_sha256" "d281b7ac55f639ff79c2cc4ab3e0afac3e4094c82cd4d675fb90db09617d406f"} {"question" "q-274673" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061747/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d67c2f25f9576853d5e88974fffe7258946afecb8c5dacf44c2234f506d47f32"} {"question" "q-274673" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061747/state.json" "file_sha256" "d281b7ac55f639ff79c2cc4ab3e0afac3e4094c82cd4d675fb90db09617d406f"} {"question" "q-274673" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-4"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061747/state.json" "file_sha256" "d281b7ac55f639ff79c2cc4ab3e0afac3e4094c82cd4d675fb90db09617d406f"} {"question" "q-274673" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061747/state.json" "file_sha256" "d281b7ac55f639ff79c2cc4ab3e0afac3e4094c82cd4d675fb90db09617d406f"} {"question" "q-274673" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "9"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061747/state.json" "file_sha256" "d281b7ac55f639ff79c2cc4ab3e0afac3e4094c82cd4d675fb90db09617d406f"} {"question" "q-274673" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061747/state.json" "file_sha256" "d281b7ac55f639ff79c2cc4ab3e0afac3e4094c82cd4d675fb90db09617d406f"} {"question" "q-274673" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-12"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061747/state.json" "file_sha256" "d281b7ac55f639ff79c2cc4ab3e0afac3e4094c82cd4d675fb90db09617d406f"} {"question" "q-16166" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061747/assets/a1959f7344a33f2bd73f00f1ef999acd0d30767b25fa41fe39dc0e7a4bda612a.png)\n\nFind the values of $t$ at which the curve $x=-{t}^{2},y=-3t$ intersects the line $x-y+4=0,$ as shown above." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061747/state.json" "file_sha256" "d281b7ac55f639ff79c2cc4ab3e0afac3e4094c82cd4d675fb90db09617d406f"} {"question" "q-16166" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that the line and curve intersect twice, so we are expecting two solutions.\n\nTo find the coordinates of the intersection points, we take the equation of the line, replace $x$ and $y$ with the parametric formulas, and then solve for $t.$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-y+4 & =0 \\\\ (-{t}^{2})-(-3t)+4 & =0 \\\\ -{t}^{2}+3t+4 & =0 \\\\ -(t+1)(t-4) & =0 \\\\ t & =-1,4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061747/state.json" "file_sha256" "d281b7ac55f639ff79c2cc4ab3e0afac3e4094c82cd4d675fb90db09617d406f"} {"question" "q-16166" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061747/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d67c2f25f9576853d5e88974fffe7258946afecb8c5dacf44c2234f506d47f32"} {"question" "q-16166" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061747/state.json" "file_sha256" "d281b7ac55f639ff79c2cc4ab3e0afac3e4094c82cd4d675fb90db09617d406f"} {"question" "q-16166" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "t=-1,3"] ["math" "t=-1,4"] ["math" "t=-1,5"] ["math" "t=-\\frac{1}{2},3"] ["math" "t=-\\frac{1}{2},4"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061747/state.json" "file_sha256" "d281b7ac55f639ff79c2cc4ab3e0afac3e4094c82cd4d675fb90db09617d406f"} {"question" "q-16166" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "t=-1,4"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061747/state.json" "file_sha256" "d281b7ac55f639ff79c2cc4ab3e0afac3e4094c82cd4d675fb90db09617d406f"} {"question" "q-102605" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061747/assets/dcac814ed1c1caf5a8796a25ccee57cc5fd988d10f24503cbf0755aeaedb5e2c.png)\n\nFind the values of $t$ at which the curve $x=8t,y=-{t}^{2}$ intersects the line $\\frac{1}{2}x-y=0,$ as shown above." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061747/state.json" "file_sha256" "d281b7ac55f639ff79c2cc4ab3e0afac3e4094c82cd4d675fb90db09617d406f"} {"question" "q-102605" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that the line and curve intersect twice, so we are expecting two solutions.\n\nTo find the coordinates of the intersection points, we take the equation of the line, replace $x$ and $y$ with the parametric formulas, and then solve for $t.$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}x-y & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(8t)-(-{t}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ 4t+{t}^{2} & =0 \\\\ t(4+t) & =0 \\\\ t & =-4,0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061747/state.json" "file_sha256" "d281b7ac55f639ff79c2cc4ab3e0afac3e4094c82cd4d675fb90db09617d406f"} {"question" "q-102605" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061747/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d67c2f25f9576853d5e88974fffe7258946afecb8c5dacf44c2234f506d47f32"} {"question" "q-102605" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061747/state.json" "file_sha256" "d281b7ac55f639ff79c2cc4ab3e0afac3e4094c82cd4d675fb90db09617d406f"} {"question" "q-102605" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "t=-2,0"] ["math" "t=-4,0"] ["math" "t=-5,0"] ["math" "t=-6,0"] ["math" "t=-8,0"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061747/state.json" "file_sha256" "d281b7ac55f639ff79c2cc4ab3e0afac3e4094c82cd4d675fb90db09617d406f"} {"question" "q-102605" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "t=-4,0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061747/state.json" "file_sha256" "d281b7ac55f639ff79c2cc4ab3e0afac3e4094c82cd4d675fb90db09617d406f"} {"question" "q-320707" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "In **ascending order**, the values of $\\theta $ at which the curve $x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi $ intersects the line $3y+x=0$ are\n\n$\\theta =$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,\\theta =$\n{{field-2}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061747/state.json" "file_sha256" "d281b7ac55f639ff79c2cc4ab3e0afac3e4094c82cd4d675fb90db09617d406f"} {"question" "q-320707" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the coordinates of the points of intersection, we take the equation of the line, replace $x$ and $y$ with the parametric formulas, and then solve for $\\theta .$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3y+x & =0 \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe principal value of $\\theta $ is\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-1)=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nThis value lies outside the domain $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .$ To get a value that's inside the domain, we add $\\pi :$\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{1}=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\pi =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\n$$\n\nTo get a second solution, we add $\\pi $ to ${\\theta }_{1}:$\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}+\\pi =\\frac{7\\pi }{4}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, our solutions are $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061747/state.json" "file_sha256" "d281b7ac55f639ff79c2cc4ab3e0afac3e4094c82cd4d675fb90db09617d406f"} {"question" "q-320707" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061747/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d67c2f25f9576853d5e88974fffe7258946afecb8c5dacf44c2234f506d47f32"} {"question" "q-320707" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061747/state.json" "file_sha256" "d281b7ac55f639ff79c2cc4ab3e0afac3e4094c82cd4d675fb90db09617d406f"} {"question" "q-320707" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{3\\pi}{4}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061747/state.json" "file_sha256" "d281b7ac55f639ff79c2cc4ab3e0afac3e4094c82cd4d675fb90db09617d406f"} {"question" "q-320707" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061747/state.json" "file_sha256" "d281b7ac55f639ff79c2cc4ab3e0afac3e4094c82cd4d675fb90db09617d406f"} {"question" "q-320707" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{7\\pi}{4}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061747/state.json" "file_sha256" "d281b7ac55f639ff79c2cc4ab3e0afac3e4094c82cd4d675fb90db09617d406f"} {"question" "q-320705" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "In **ascending order**, the values of $\\theta $ at which the curve $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi $ intersects the line $y+2\\sqrt{3}\\,x=0$ are\n\n$\\theta =$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,\\theta =$\n{{field-2}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061747/state.json" "file_sha256" "d281b7ac55f639ff79c2cc4ab3e0afac3e4094c82cd4d675fb90db09617d406f"} {"question" "q-320705" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the coordinates of the points of intersection, we take the equation of the line, replace $x$ and $y$ with the parametric formulas, and then solve for $\\theta .$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+2\\sqrt{3}\\,x & =0 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2\\sqrt{3}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =-2\\sqrt{3}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{-2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =-\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe principal value of $\\theta $ is\n\n$$\n\\theta =arctan\\,(-\\sqrt{3})=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nThis value lies outside the domain $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .$ To get a value that's inside the domain, we add $\\pi :$\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{1}=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\pi =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\n$$\n\nTo get a second solution, we add $\\pi $ to ${\\theta }_{1}:$\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+\\pi =\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, our solutions are $\\theta =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{3}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061747/state.json" "file_sha256" "d281b7ac55f639ff79c2cc4ab3e0afac3e4094c82cd4d675fb90db09617d406f"} {"question" "q-320705" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061747/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d67c2f25f9576853d5e88974fffe7258946afecb8c5dacf44c2234f506d47f32"} {"question" "q-320705" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061747/state.json" "file_sha256" "d281b7ac55f639ff79c2cc4ab3e0afac3e4094c82cd4d675fb90db09617d406f"} {"question" "q-320705" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{2\\pi}{3}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061747/state.json" "file_sha256" "d281b7ac55f639ff79c2cc4ab3e0afac3e4094c82cd4d675fb90db09617d406f"} {"question" "q-320705" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061747/state.json" "file_sha256" "d281b7ac55f639ff79c2cc4ab3e0afac3e4094c82cd4d675fb90db09617d406f"} {"question" "q-320705" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{5\\pi}{3}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061747/state.json" "file_sha256" "d281b7ac55f639ff79c2cc4ab3e0afac3e4094c82cd4d675fb90db09617d406f"} {"question" "q-16178" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the values of $\\theta $ at which the curve $x=\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi $ intersects the line $\\sqrt{3}x+y=0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061747/state.json" "file_sha256" "d281b7ac55f639ff79c2cc4ab3e0afac3e4094c82cd4d675fb90db09617d406f"} {"question" "q-16178" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the coordinates of the points of intersection, we take the equation of the line, replace $x$ and $y$ with the parametric formulas, and then solve for $\\theta :$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3}x+y & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt{3}(\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe principal value of $\\theta $ is\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\n$$\n\nThis value lies outside the domain $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .$ To get a value that's inside the domain, we add $\\pi :$\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{1}=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\pi =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\n$$\n\nTo get a second solution, we add $\\pi $ to ${\\theta }_{1}:$\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}+\\pi =\\frac{11\\pi }{6}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our solutions are $\\frac{5\\pi }{6},\\,\\frac{11\\pi }{6}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061747/state.json" "file_sha256" "d281b7ac55f639ff79c2cc4ab3e0afac3e4094c82cd4d675fb90db09617d406f"} {"question" "q-16178" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061747/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d67c2f25f9576853d5e88974fffe7258946afecb8c5dacf44c2234f506d47f32"} {"question" "q-16178" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061747/state.json" "file_sha256" "d281b7ac55f639ff79c2cc4ab3e0afac3e4094c82cd4d675fb90db09617d406f"} {"question" "q-16178" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{2\\pi }{3},\\,\\frac{11\\pi }{6}"] ["math" "\\frac{2\\pi }{3},\\,\\frac{5\\pi }{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{5\\pi }{6},\\,\\frac{11\\pi }{6}"] ["math" "\\frac{5\\pi }{6},\\,\\frac{4\\pi }{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{5\\pi }{6},\\,\\frac{5\\pi }{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061747/state.json" "file_sha256" "d281b7ac55f639ff79c2cc4ab3e0afac3e4094c82cd4d675fb90db09617d406f"} {"question" "q-16178" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{5\\pi }{6},\\,\\frac{11\\pi }{6}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061747/state.json" "file_sha256" "d281b7ac55f639ff79c2cc4ab3e0afac3e4094c82cd4d675fb90db09617d406f"} {"question" "q-320696" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "In **ascending order**, the values of $\\theta $ at which the curve $x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi $ intersects the line $2\\sqrt{3}\\,y=x$ are\n\n$\\theta =$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,\\theta =$\n{{field-2}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061747/state.json" "file_sha256" "d281b7ac55f639ff79c2cc4ab3e0afac3e4094c82cd4d675fb90db09617d406f"} {"question" "q-320696" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the coordinates of the points of intersection, we take the equation of the line, replace $x$ and $y$ with the parametric formulas, and then solve for $\\theta .$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{3}\\,y & =x \\\\ 2\\sqrt{3}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{3}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe principal value of $\\theta $ is\n\n$$\n\\theta =arctan\\,(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\n$$\n\nThis value lies inside the domain $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ so our first solution is ${\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\nTo get a second solution, we add $\\pi $ to ${\\theta }_{1}:$\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\pi =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, our solutions are $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061747/state.json" "file_sha256" "d281b7ac55f639ff79c2cc4ab3e0afac3e4094c82cd4d675fb90db09617d406f"} {"question" "q-320696" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061747/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d67c2f25f9576853d5e88974fffe7258946afecb8c5dacf44c2234f506d47f32"} {"question" "q-320696" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061747/state.json" "file_sha256" "d281b7ac55f639ff79c2cc4ab3e0afac3e4094c82cd4d675fb90db09617d406f"} {"question" "q-320696" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\pi}{6}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061747/state.json" "file_sha256" "d281b7ac55f639ff79c2cc4ab3e0afac3e4094c82cd4d675fb90db09617d406f"} {"question" "q-320696" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061747/state.json" "file_sha256" "d281b7ac55f639ff79c2cc4ab3e0afac3e4094c82cd4d675fb90db09617d406f"} {"question" "q-320696" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{7\\pi}{6}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061747/state.json" "file_sha256" "d281b7ac55f639ff79c2cc4ab3e0afac3e4094c82cd4d675fb90db09617d406f"} {"question" "q-102633" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the values of $\\theta $ at which the curve $x=5\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi $ intersects the line $x-5y=0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061747/state.json" "file_sha256" "d281b7ac55f639ff79c2cc4ab3e0afac3e4094c82cd4d675fb90db09617d406f"} {"question" "q-102633" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the coordinates of the points of intersection, we take the equation of the line, replace $x$ and $y$ with the parametric formulas, and then solve for $\\theta .$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-5y & =0 \\\\ 5\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ 5\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe principal value of $\\theta $ is\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3})=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nThis value lies inside the domain $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ so our first solution is ${\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\nTo get a second solution, we add $\\pi $ to ${\\theta }_{1}:$\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\pi =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our solutions are $\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\,\\frac{4\\pi }{3}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061747/state.json" "file_sha256" "d281b7ac55f639ff79c2cc4ab3e0afac3e4094c82cd4d675fb90db09617d406f"} {"question" "q-102633" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061747/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d67c2f25f9576853d5e88974fffe7258946afecb8c5dacf44c2234f506d47f32"} {"question" "q-102633" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061747/state.json" "file_sha256" "d281b7ac55f639ff79c2cc4ab3e0afac3e4094c82cd4d675fb90db09617d406f"} {"question" "q-102633" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\,\\frac{2\\pi }{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\,\\frac{4\\pi }{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{5\\pi }{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\,\\frac{7\\pi }{6}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061747/state.json" "file_sha256" "d281b7ac55f639ff79c2cc4ab3e0afac3e4094c82cd4d675fb90db09617d406f"} {"question" "q-102633" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\,\\frac{4\\pi }{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061747/state.json" "file_sha256" "d281b7ac55f639ff79c2cc4ab3e0afac3e4094c82cd4d675fb90db09617d406f"} {"question" "e-5373" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the points where the curve $x=t^2,y=2t$ intersects the line $4x-2y-15=0.$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061747/assets/ba682be1ab26bad257fe814020469696f5c302a765a67c45c341762ac9418108.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061747/example-5373.json" "file_sha256" "d7efe10b28030bfb43ea55dbd8fc285a9feda69d78d7fd03e2be611bf1226476"} {"question" "e-5373" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that the line and curve intersect twice, so we are expecting two solutions.\n\nTo find the coordinates of intersection points, we take the equation of the line, replace $x$ and $y$ with the parametric formulas, and then solve for $t.$ Upon finding $t,$ we then proceed to find the coordinates of the intersection points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x-2y-15 & =0 \\\\ 4({t}^{2})-2(2t)-15 & =0 \\\\ 4{t}^{2}-4t-15 & =0 \\\\ (2t-5)(2t+3) & =0 \\\\ t & =\\frac{5}{2},-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we have the values of $t,$ we proceed to find $x$ and $y\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n- We start with $t=\\dfrac{5}{2}.$ For $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={t}^{2} \\\\ x & ={(\\frac{5}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2t \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{5}{2} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the coordinates of the first intersection point are $\\left(\\dfrac{25}{4},5\\right).$\n\n- We now move over to $t=-\\dfrac{3}{2}.$ For $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={t}^{2} \\\\ x & ={(-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2t \\\\  & =2(-\\frac{3}{2}) \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the coordinates of the second intersection point are $\\left(\\dfrac{9}{4},-3\\right).$\n\nTherefore, the curve $x=t^2,y=2t$ intersects the line $4x-2y-15=0$  at the points $\\left(\\dfrac{25}{4},5\\right)$ and $\\left(\\dfrac{9}{4},-3\\right).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061747/example-5373.json" "file_sha256" "d7efe10b28030bfb43ea55dbd8fc285a9feda69d78d7fd03e2be611bf1226476"} {"question" "e-5372" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the values of $\\theta$ at which the curve $x=\\cos \\theta, \\, y = 2 \\sin \\theta, \\, 0 \\leq \\theta < 2\\pi$ intersects the line $2x-y=0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061747/example-5372.json" "file_sha256" "f2ce3d14a6b841e22c1980a5b67fa51324dbc38c401ee8b2f9f149152ff0846e"} {"question" "e-5372" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the coordinates of the points of intersection, we take the equation of the line, replace $x$ and $y$ with the parametric formulas, and then solve for $\\theta.$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-y & =0 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 1 & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\ 1 & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe principal value of $\\theta$ is\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\arctan(1) = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}.\n$$\n\nThis value lies inside the domain $0\\leq \\theta <     2\\pi,$ so our first solution is $\\theta_1 = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}.$\n\nTo get a second solution, we add $\\pi$ to $\\theta_1\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\theta_1 = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4} + \\pi = \\dfrac{5\\pi}{4}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our solutions are $\\dfrac{\\pi}{4},\\,\\dfrac{5\\pi}{4}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061747/example-5372.json" "file_sha256" "f2ce3d14a6b841e22c1980a5b67fa51324dbc38c401ee8b2f9f149152ff0846e"}] "usage" {"e-5372" [] "e-5373" []} "basis" 1978 "retractions" [[17592186100641 :question/problem "Find the points where the curve $x=t^2,y=2t$ intersects the line $4x-2y-15=0$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1257/Source/Images/157c9e3533adbdf48403abc3e6cb25b9.png)"] [17592186100641 :question/worked-solution "Notice that the line and curve intersect twice, so we are expecting two solutions.\n\nTo find the coordinates of intersection points, we take the equation of the line, replace $x$ and $y$ with the parametric formulas, and then solve for $t$. Upon finding $t$, we then proceed to find the coordinates of the intersection points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4x - 2y - 15 &= 0 \\\\\n4(t^{2}) - 2(2t) - 15 &= 0 \\\\\n4t^{2} - 4t - 15 &= 0 \\\\\n(2t - 5)(2t + 3) &= 0 \\\\\nt &= \\frac{5}{2},-\\frac{3}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we have the values of $t$, we proceed to find $x$ and $y$:\n\n- We start with $t=\\dfrac{5}{2}$. For $x$, we have\n$x|= t^{2}; x|= (\\frac{5}{2})^{2}; = \\frac{25}{4}$.\nFor $y$, we have\n$y|= 2t; = 2 \\cdot \\frac{5}{2}; = 5$.\nSo the coordinates of the first intersection point are $\\left(\\dfrac{25}{4},5\\right)$.\n- We now move over to $t=-\\dfrac{3}{2}$. For $x$, we have\n$x|= t^{2}; x|= (-\\frac{3}{2})^{2}; = \\frac{9}{4}$.\nFor $y$, we have\n$y|= 2t; = 2(-\\frac{3}{2}); =-3$.\nSo the coordinates of the second intersection point are $\\left(\\dfrac{9}{4},-3\\right)$.\n\nTherefore, the curve $x=t^2,y=2t$ intersects the line $4x-2y-15=0$ at the points\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{25}{4},5\\right)\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{9}{4},-3\\right)\n$$"] [17592186100640 :question/problem "Find the values of $\\theta$ at which the curve $x=\\cos \\theta, \\, y = 2 \\sin \\theta, \\, 0 \\leq \\theta < 2\\pi$ intersects the line $2x-y=0$."] [17592186100640 :question/worked-solution "To find the coordinates of the points of intersection, we take the equation of the line, replace $x$ and $y$ with the parametric formulas, and then solve for $\\theta$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x - y &= 0 \\\\\n2\\cos θ - 2\\sin θ &= 0 \\\\\n2\\cos θ &= 2\\sin θ \\\\\n\\cos θ &= \\sin θ \\\\\n1 &= \\frac{\\sin θ}{\\cos θ} \\\\\n1 &= \\tan θ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe principal value of $\\theta$ is\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\arctan(1) = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\n$$\n\nThis value lies inside the domain\n\n$$\n0\\leq \\theta < 2\\pi\n$$\n\nso our first solution is\n\n$$\n\\theta_1 = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\n$$\n\nTo get a second solution, we add $\\pi$ to $\\theta_1$:\n\n$$\n\\theta_1 = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4} + \\pi = \\dfrac{5\\pi}{4}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our solutions are\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\pi}{4},\\,\\dfrac{5\\pi}{4}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [] "immutable_fields" [] "no_history" []}
